---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A single line growing into a bigram, a trigram, and a hexagram, left to right, with one hexagram line picked out in gold as a changing line"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Build a visual sign system from its smallest unit upward — a single
    yin/yang line, then bigrams, trigrams, and hexagrams — and use the
    changing-line rule to represent transformation directly in the notation.
    In the second half of the semester, trace how the meanings and images
    layered onto that notation developed, and were sometimes conflated,
    across history: the <em>Zhouyi</em>'s composite text, two different
    trigram arrangements, and two historically separate images both called
    "Taijitu." You leave with the ability to tell a well-sourced historical
    claim about a sign system from an overstated one, and to apply the same
    toolkit to a system you've never seen.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone interested in how visual notations work, with no background in
    Chinese language, philosophy, or history required — the course teaches
    what it needs as it goes, and is explicit about which claims are
    historically documented, which are traditional attribution, and which
    are later interpretation. It asks for comfort reading contested academic
    sourcing critically, since that discipline runs through every week.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
